3. Configure the logging configuration in `application.properties` to include log levels for all packages:
```properties
logging.level.org.springframework=DEBUG
logging.level.com.example=INFO
```
This will ensure that logs from all packages (including those not explicitly configured) are included and can be easily filtered or analyzed.

4. Create a custom logging filter to exclude sensitive data from logs, such as credit card numbers:
```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class SensitiveDataFilter implements Filter {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(SensitiveDataFilter.class);

    @Override
    public boolean shouldInclude(String logMessage) {
        // exclude sensitive data from logs, such as credit card numbers
        return !logMessage.contains("creditCardNumber");
    }
}
```
This filter can be applied to the logging configuration to ensure that sensitive data is not included in logs.

5. Implement a log rotation policy using Log4j 2 to rotate logs every 30 days:
```xml
<Configuration status="INFO">
    <Appenders>
        <ConsoleAppender name="console" target="SYSTEM_OUT">
            <PatternLayout pattern="%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss} [%t] %-5level %logger{36} - %msg%n"/>
        </ConsoleAppender>
        <FileAppender name="file" fileName="logs/app.log" filter="SensitiveDataFilter">
            <PatternLayout pattern="%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss} [%t] %-5level %logger{36} - %msg%n"/>
        </FileAppender>
    </Appenders>
    <Loggers>
        <Root level="INFO">
            <AppenderRef ref="console"/>
            <AppenderRef ref="file"/>
        </Root>
    </Loggers>
</Configuration>
```
This will ensure that logs are rotated regularly and do not grow indefinitely.

